{
  "id": "lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-tree",
  "questionId": "lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-tree",
  "slug": "lowest-common-ancestor-of-a-binary-tree",
  "title": "Lowest Common Ancestor of a Binary Tree",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree dfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree",
    "dfs",
    "recursion"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "grind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "if you already knew, for every subtree, whether it contains p, whether it contains q, or both, how would you decide the answer at the current node?",
    "write a recursive helper on an index that returns the LCA value found within that subtree, or a sentinel meaning 'not found here'",
    "at each node: if the node's own value is p or q, that node is itself a candidate answer (since one of the two live at or below it) - return it directly",
    "otherwise recurse into both children; if both sides report finding something, the current node is the LCA; if only one side reports something, pass that result upward unchanged"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "assuming this is a binary search tree and comparing values to decide direction, when the tree here is a general binary tree with no ordering guarantee",
    "not handling the case where one of p or q is an ancestor of the other, returning some node further up instead of p or q itself",
    "forgetting to check both 'index out of bounds' and 'value equals -101' as the missing-node base case",
    "returning as soon as one child search succeeds without checking whether the other child also succeeds, which misses the case where the true LCA is the current node"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Binary trees in these problems are encoded as a flat array named `tree`\nusing complete-binary-tree (heap) indexing:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`\n- `-101` means \"no node there\"\n\nAll node values in a given tree are distinct, and you're given two\nintegers `p` and `q`, each guaranteed to equal the value of some node\nin the tree. For example, `[3, 5, 1, 6, 2, 0, 8, -101, -101, 7, 4]`\nencodes this tree:\n\n```\n            3\n          /   \\\n         5     1\n        / \\   / \\\n       6   2 0   8\n          / \\\n         7   4\n```\n\nHere, the lowest common ancestor of `p = 5` and `q = 1` is the root,\n`3` (one lives in its left subtree, the other in its right subtree),\nwhile the lowest common ancestor of `p = 5` and `q = 4` is `5` itself,\nsince 4 is 5's own descendant.\n\n**Return the value of the lowest common ancestor of `p` and `q`: the deepest node that has a descendant valued `p` and a descendant valued `q` somewhere in its subtree.**\n\nA node counts as a descendant of itself, so if `p` is itself an ancestor\nof `q`, `p` is the answer.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= number of nodes in tree <= 500",
      "-100 <= tree[i] <= 100 for every real node",
      "all real node values in a given tree are distinct",
      "p != q",
      "both p and q are guaranteed to be the value of some node present in tree"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "p is an ancestor of q (or q is an ancestor of p), so the answer is p or q itself rather than some node above both",
      "p and q are the two children of the root, so the root is the answer",
      "the tree has only two nodes total"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 5, 1, 6, 2, 0, 8, -101, -101, 7, 4], p = 5, q = 1",
        "output": "3",
        "expectedOutput": "3",
        "explanation": "node 5 lives in the root's left subtree and node 1 lives in its right subtree, so the root (3) is their lowest common ancestor"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 5, 1, 6, 2, 0, 8, -101, -101, 7, 4], p = 5, q = 4",
        "output": "5",
        "expectedOutput": "5",
        "explanation": "node 4 is a descendant of node 5, so node 5 itself is the lowest common ancestor"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2], p = 1, q = 2",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "the root is an ancestor of every other node, including its only child"
      }
    ],
